(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-medical-imaging-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Principles of Medical Imaging Quiz 2 — PDM I Exam 1","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the decubitus position used to evaluate?","choices":["Aortic calcification, because gravity displaces the overlying bowel gas out of the field","Pleural effusion, because gravity lets the fluid around the lung level out and be seen","Free air under the diaphragm, because gravity lets the air rise to the highest point","Bowel obstruction, because gravity separates the air from the fluid within the loops"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — fluid in the pleural space moves when the patient is laid on their side. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which disadvantages are named for conventional radiography?","choices":["Operator dependence and an inability to penetrate bone or large gas-filled structures","Long scan times and safety problems with magnetic implanted devices","Very high radiation dose and an inability to be made portable at all","A limited range of densities and reliance on ionizing radiation, though at relatively low dose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the dose is low compared with other radiographic modalities. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which term describes tissue that appears white because it absorbs more of the beam?","choices":["Isodense, matching the density of the surrounding tissue","Attenuated, meaning the beam has been scattered away","Radiopaque, also called hyperdense or radiodense","Radiolucent, also called hypodense"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — less passage of x-ray photons produces the whiter appearance. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is unique about radiation exposure in nuclear medicine compared with other modalities that use ionizing radiation?","choices":["The exposure is delivered entirely in a single pulse rather than over a continuous scan","The exposure is confined to the organ being studied rather than reaching adjacent tissue","The exposure is measured in different units from those used for conventional radiography","The patient can briefly become the source of radiation exposure to other people"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the tracer is inside the patient, so the emission travels with them. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which safety issues are named for magnetic resonance imaging?","choices":["Magnetic implanted devices and ferromagnetic projectiles, and it is still not recommended in pregnancy or infants","Cumulative ionizing dose and radiation-sensitive organs, and it is still not recommended in pregnancy or infants","Nephrotoxicity and anaphylaxis to iodine, and it is still not recommended in renal impairment","Operator dependence and poor penetration of bone, and it is still not recommended in obesity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — no radiation is emitted, but the magnet itself creates hazards. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"How is computed tomography's place among imaging modalities described?","choices":["It is the cornerstone of neuroimaging specifically","It is the most widely obtained imaging study performed","It is the cornerstone of cross-sectional imaging","It is the safest of all the radioimaging modalities"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — magnetic resonance imaging is described as the cornerstone of neuroimaging. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which four questions frame the choice of an imaging modality?","choices":["Which modality is cheapest, which is fastest, which is closest, and which the patient prefers","Which modality the radiologist prefers, which is available today, which the insurer covers, and which is quickest","Which modality gives the highest resolution, the largest field of view, the thinnest slices, and the fastest overall scan time for the patient","Which modality best rules the diagnosis in or out, is there a lower-radiation alternative, what is the risk against benefit for this patient, and is contrast needed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — those are the four the lecture opens with. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which oral contrast agent is contraindicated when perforation is suspected, and what is used instead?","choices":["Barium is contraindicated because it is toxic to extra-intestinal tissue; use gastrografin","Gastrografin is contraindicated because it is toxic to extra-intestinal tissue; use barium","Iohexol is contraindicated because it is toxic to extra-intestinal tissue; use barium","Both are contraindicated because either is toxic to extra-intestinal tissue; use none"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — barium outside the bowel causes alkaline burns. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"On the Hounsfield scale, where water is zero, which tissue sits at roughly minus forty to minus one hundred and twenty?","choices":["Air, which is why it reads darker than every other tissue on the scan","Fat, which is why it reads darker than soft tissue but lighter than air","Soft tissue, which is why it reads darker than fat but lighter than water","Bone, which is why it reads brighter than soft tissue but darker than metal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — fat is negative, but nowhere near as negative as air. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is a computed tomography image composed of, and what is each unit assigned?","choices":["A series of two-dimensional projections, each one assigned its own radiodensity value as measured in millisieverts","A hydrogen map, each point assigned a relaxation time in milliseconds reflecting the local chemical environment of that tissue","A matrix of thousands of tiny squares called pixels, each assigned a computed tomography number from −1000 to +1000 in Hounsfield units","A matrix of thousands of tiny cubes called voxels, each assigned a greyscale signal intensity on a fixed scale running from zero to two hundred and fifty five"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — water is assigned a Hounsfield number of zero by convention. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which tissues is magnetic resonance imaging described as the best modality for?","choices":["Bone and calcified structures, essentially anything mineralised, with high spatial detail","Air-containing structures, essentially the lungs and sinuses, with high contrast detail","Metabolically active tissue, essentially tumour and infection, with high functional detail","Soft tissue, essentially anything other than bone, with extremely high anatomical detail"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deck states it in exactly those terms. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which three standard planes are used in cross-sectional imaging, and how does each divide the body?","choices":["Axial into upper and lower, coronal into right and left, sagittal into anterior and posterior","Axial into upper and lower, coronal into anterior and posterior, sagittal into right and left","Axial into anterior and posterior, coronal into right and left, sagittal into upper and lower","Axial into right and left, coronal into upper and lower, sagittal into anterior and posterior"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — axial is also called transverse and is the commonest of the three. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What must be checked before giving intravenous iodinated contrast, and why?","choices":["Blood urea nitrogen and creatinine, because the agent is nephrotoxic","Liver function tests and bilirubin, because the agent is cleared hepatically","Thyroid function tests, because the iodine load can precipitate thyrotoxicosis","Complete blood count and platelets, because the agent affects coagulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and a litre of normal saline is given to protect the kidneys. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Who is credited with developing the first computed tomography scanner, and what recognition followed?","choices":["Paul Lauterbur, who won the Nobel Prize for developing magnetic resonance imaging","Sir Godfrey Hounsfield, who won the 1979 Nobel Prize in Medicine along with Allan Cormack","Wilhelm Roentgen, who won the first Nobel Prize for Physics in 1901","Allan Cormack alone, who won the 1979 Nobel Prize for Physics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the unit of attenuation is named after him. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which patients are described as high risk before contrast administration?","choices":["Patients with a documented anaphylactic reaction to any medication","Patients with a documented allergy to shellfish or to dietary iodine","Patients with a documented reaction to any topical antiseptic containing iodine","Patients with a documented history of asthma or of seasonal allergic rhinitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the history that matters is anaphylaxis to anything, not to iodine alone. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"How are radiographic projections named?","choices":["In the direction the patient faces, from the side nearest the film to the side furthest away","In the order the structures appear, from the most superficial down to the deepest tissue","In the position of the detector, from the cassette's near edge to its furthest corner","In the direction the beam travels, from what it strikes first to the most distal portion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — so posterior-anterior means the beam enters the back and exits the front. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"On the Hounsfield scale, where water is assigned zero, what value is air given?","choices":["Minus one thousand, the bottom of the scale","Minus one hundred and twenty, the bottom of the scale","Plus one thousand, the top of the scale","Plus four hundred, the upper middle of the scale"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — air absorbs least, so it takes the most negative number. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"In that same table, which listed study delivers the lowest organ dose?","choices":["Screening mammography, to the breast","Dental radiography, to the brain","Posterior-anterior chest radiography, to the lung","Lateral chest radiography, to the lung"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — five thousandths, the smallest figure in the table. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does the deck's three-panel chest example illustrate about post-processing?","choices":["The same anatomy imaged on three different modalities, compared for their relative strengths","Three consecutive time points after contrast, compared to show the pattern of enhancement","The same acquired data re-windowed to bring out different pathology, without re-scanning the patient","Three separate acquisitions at different dose settings, compared side by side for quality"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that is the benefit digital imaging markedly advanced. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How do computed tomography angiography and magnetic resonance angiography differ in their use of dye?","choices":["Computed tomography angiography injects iodine quickly; magnetic resonance angiography needs no dye","Computed tomography angiography needs no dye; magnetic resonance angiography injects gadolinium quickly","Both inject iodine, but computed tomography angiography uses a much lower concentration","Neither injects dye, because both reconstruct the vessels from the raw data afterwards"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — magnetic resonance angiography images the arterial walls without contrast. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Why does the deck stress giving the radiologist relevant clinical information?","choices":["The radiologist repeats the study otherwise, so the history prevents duplicate imaging","The radiologist has not seen the patient, so the history is what guides their read","The radiologist bills separately, so the indication must be documented for the claim","The radiologist selects the modality, so the history determines which scanner is booked"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the report is only as good as the question it is answering. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What determines the frequency of the radio waves a magnetic resonance scanner reads?","choices":["The density of the tissue in Hounsfield units, together with the chosen window","The metabolic rate of the tissue, together with the dose of tracer given","The chemical environment of the hydrogen atoms, together with their location","The strength of the magnet in Tesla, together with the duration of the scan"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both, which is what lets the computer build a spatial map. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Why can magnetic resonance imaging show tissues that are surrounded by bone better than computed tomography can?","choices":["Calcium emits no signal, so the bone does not obscure what sits inside it","Calcium emits a strong signal, so the bone can be subtracted from the image afterwards","Bone is windowed out during acquisition, so only the soft tissue range is displayed","Bone absorbs the radio waves, so the surrounding soft tissue returns a relatively brighter signal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — silence from calcium is an advantage rather than a gap. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What do the terms radiolucent and hypodense describe?","choices":["A whiter image, from less passage of x-ray photons through the tissue and therefore greater absorption","A brighter signal on a T2-weighted magnetic resonance image of the same tissue","A region of increased uptake of an injected radioisotope on nuclear imaging","A darker image, from greater passage of x-ray photons through the tissue and therefore less absorption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — radiopaque and hyperdense describe the opposite. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which imaging devices does the deck flag as the highest-emitting currently in existence?","choices":["Magnetic resonance imaging, magnetic resonance angiography and magnetic resonance venography","Ultrasonography, colour Doppler ultrasonography and the hand-held bedside ultrasound units","Computed tomography, positron emission tomography and single photon emission tomography","Conventional radiography, fluoroscopy and the angiographic studies performed with x-rays"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck marks this IMPORTANT, and all three use ionizing radiation. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is oral contrast indicated for?","choices":["Structure or disease of the intraluminal space, anywhere from the upper oesophagus to the rectum","Structure or disease of the vascular space, anywhere from the aortic root to the digital arteries","Structure or disease of the pleural space, anywhere from the apex to the costophrenic angle","Structure or disease of the urinary space, anywhere from the renal pelvis to the urethra"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it outlines the lumen of the gastrointestinal tract. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What practical ordering point does the deck make about imaging several body regions?","choices":["Multiple body parts require the radiologist to countersign the request before booking","Multiple body parts must be imaged on separate days to limit cumulative radiation dose","Multiple body parts or sections may mean multiple orders, giving brain, cervical, thoracic and lumbar spine as the example","Multiple body parts should be combined into one single order, so that the patient is scanned once and the radiologist reads the whole study together"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — one clinical question can require four separate requests. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What does fluoroscopy let a clinician evaluate?","choices":["Direction and velocity of blood flow, viewed as colour superimposed on grey scale","Motion of body parts and positioning changes of bones and joints, viewed in real time","Metabolic activity in organs and tissues, viewed as intensities after tracer injection","Water content in soft tissue and oedema, viewed on differently weighted sequences"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — real-time visualisation is the point of the study. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which density appears blackest on a conventional radiograph, and why?","choices":["Metal, because it absorbs the most x-rays of any material","Calcium, because it is the densest naturally occurring material","Fat, because it is less dense than the surrounding soft tissue","Air, because it absorbs the least x-rays"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — greater passage of photons means a darker image. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How does the deck summarise ultrasound's safety?","choices":["Safe once screened, though implanted metallic devices must be excluded beforehand","Very safe, without any known major side effects at medically diagnostic levels","Broadly safe, though a cumulative dose limit applies across repeated examinations","Safe in adults, though it is avoided in pregnancy and in infants as a precaution"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is called the safest of these modalities. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 24"}]};
